  "question": {
    "text": "<p>In the given circuit, the magnitude of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>C</mi></msub></math> are twice that of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub></math>. Given that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>f</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>50</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>Hz</mi></math>, the inductance of the coil is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>K</mi><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi>mH</mi></math>. The value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi></math> is _____ .</p><p><img src=\"assets\\qt0077_f0eb299fdcde.png\" style=\"width: 250px; height: 210px;\" /></p>"
  },
  "options": [],
  "correct_answer": "0",
  "solution": {
    "text": "<p>Since the voltage across the inductor&nbsp;and capacitor is same, the circuit is in resonance. Therefore, the current in the circuit will be,</p><p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>i</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>220</mn><mn>5</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>44</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math>.</p><p>The voltage across the inductor is double that across the resistor. Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>R</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mn>440</mn><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>.</p><p>We know that&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>L</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#960;</mi><mi>f</mi><mi>L</mi><mo>&#8658;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>440</mn><mrow><mn>44</mn><mo>&#215;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>&#960;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#160;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>100</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">&#960;</mi></mfrac><mi>mH</mi></math></p><p>Therefore,&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>K</mi><mo>=</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>100</mn></mfrac><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>01</mn><mo>&#8776;</mo><mn>0</mn></math>.</p>"
  }
}